The sum of distances from any point $(m,n)$ on the ellipse to the two foci is constant. We can compute this constant by using the fact that (\textit{for example}) the sum of distances from $(m,n)$ to the foci is the same as the sum of distances from one of the foci, $(0,2)$, to the origin, $(0,0)$, and from $(m,n)$ to the other focus, $(3,0)$. We have \begin{align*}
|m| &+ \sqrt{n^2 + (m-3)^2} = |0-2| + |0-0| + \sqrt{(m-0)^2 + (n-2)^2}
\\ &= 2 + \sqrt{(m-0)^2 + (0-2)^2}
\\ &= 2 + \sqrt{m^2 + 4}.
\end{align*}On the other hand, $|m| + \sqrt{n^2 + (m - 3)^2}$ is the sum of the distances from $(m, n)$ to the two foci $F_1$ and $F_2.$ Thus we have the constant $|m| + \sqrt{n^2 + (m-3)^2} = 2 + \sqrt{m^2 + 4}.$</think> <answer>$(3, 2)$</answer>

